<h2>Why we look twice</h2>
<p>The first camera run finds the problem. But a line packed with roots, grease or rust scale hides some of what's wrong with it. Once the jetter has cleared it, a second look sometimes turns up a crack behind a root mass, a joint that has pulled apart, or a low spot that only shows once the standing water drains. Now and then that changes the plan from lining to bursting or a dig. Better to learn that before a liner cures in place than after.</p>

<h2>What we need from you</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A way into the line.</strong> An existing cleanout is ideal. If there isn't one, we'll find the line and set up access.</li>
  <li><strong>Several hours without using the drains</strong> on most home lining jobs. You'll know the start and finish times ahead of time.</li>
  <li><strong>A decision on surface restoration</strong> wherever an access pit is needed, agreed on before the work starts.</li>
  <li><strong>For rentals, HOAs and commercial buildings:</strong> help with tenant notices and access to each area as the work moves through it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What we won't do</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Quote a lining job without running a camera through the line first</li>
  <li>Line a pipe that has sagged, lost its slope or collapsed</li>
  <li>Install a liner over roots or scale to save cleaning time</li>
  <li>Skip the permit or the city or district inspection</li>
  <li>Leave without showing you the finished pipe on camera</li>
</ul>
<p>If a sewer quote you've been given skipped any of these, a second opinion is worth it. <a href="/sewer-camera-inspection/">Start with a camera inspection</a>.</p>
